1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,500
For most of human history, the most dangerous thing an ordinary person did — more dangerous
than war, more dangerous than travel, more dangerous than any job — was have a baby.

2
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:13,650
And half the species did it,
on average, six or seven times.

3
00:00:13,650 --> 00:00:15,730
There was no anaesthetic.

4
00:00:15,730 --> 00:00:17,200
No antibiotics.

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:18,360
No transfusion.

6
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:20,060
No surgery you could survive.

7
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:24,940
If something went wrong, there was almost nothing anybody
could do except be in the room with you.

8
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:27,060
So what was that actually like?

9
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:28,990
Not the statistics.

10
00:00:28,990 --> 00:00:30,110
The room.

11
00:00:30,110 --> 00:00:31,270
The hours.

12
00:00:31,270 --> 00:00:32,530
The thing itself.

13
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:38,520
Let's get the scale right first, because it usually gets
exaggerated in one direction and then dismissed in the other.

14
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:43,280
In pre-industrial Europe, roughly one birth
in a hundred killed the mother.

15
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:45,760
One percent sounds survivable.

16
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:55,770
And for any single birth, it was — the overwhelming majority of women came through it
fine, which matters, because the version of history where everyone was constantly dying is wrong.

17
00:00:55,770 --> 00:00:57,960
But nobody had one birth.

18
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:00,590
Six or seven pregnancies was ordinary.

19
00:01:00,590 --> 00:01:07,220
Run one percent across seven births and the lifetime
risk lands somewhere around five to ten percent.

20
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:10,960
Roughly one woman in fifteen
died of it, eventually.

21
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:15,770
Which means this: every single woman in a
village knew someone it had happened to.

22
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:17,000
Not as a story.

23
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:22,030
As a person, by name, whose children
were now being raised by somebody else.

24
00:01:22,030 --> 00:01:25,710
And that is the fact that sets
the emotional temperature of the whole thing.

25
00:01:25,710 --> 00:01:27,630
The fear wasn't superstition.

26
00:01:27,630 --> 00:01:28,910
The fear was accurate.

27
00:01:28,910 --> 00:01:35,260
Here's the part that surprises people, because we picture
something cold and medical, and it was the opposite.

28
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,610
There was no doctor.

29
00:01:36,610 --> 00:01:40,660
For almost all of history, birth was
not a medical event at all.

30
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:42,280
It was a female one.

31
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:46,250
When labour started, word went
out and the women came.

32
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:54,730
A midwife, if the village had a good one — and a good
midwife was somebody with decades of experience and hundreds of births behind her.

33
00:01:54,730 --> 00:01:58,060
Plus your mother, your
sisters, your neighbours.

34
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:00,830
There's a lovely bit of etymology here.

35
00:02:00,830 --> 00:02:04,170
The women who attended a
birth were called your gossips.

36
00:02:04,170 --> 00:02:09,900
It comes from god-sibling, meaning your spiritual relatives
— the people close enough to be present.

37
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:17,170
Only later did the word slide into meaning idle talk, because of
what those women did in that room for hours on end.

38
00:02:17,170 --> 00:02:19,350
And they did talk.

39
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:20,560
That was the point.

40
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:23,750
The room itself was kept warm and dark.

41
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:27,490
Windows covered, fire going,
sometimes for days.

42
00:02:27,490 --> 00:02:29,690
Food and drink kept coming.

43
00:02:29,690 --> 00:02:33,430
There was singing in some
traditions, prayer in most.

44
00:02:33,430 --> 00:02:35,720
You were not alone for a second.

45
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:42,770
That was not an accident and it was not sentimentality
— it was the single most effective thing anyone had.

46
00:02:42,770 --> 00:02:52,080
There is a mountain of modern research showing that continuous support during
labour shortens it, reduces complications and reduces the need for intervention.

47
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:53,730
They didn't know why it worked.

48
00:02:53,730 --> 00:02:55,590
They just never stopped doing it.

49
00:02:55,590 --> 00:02:57,480
So, practically.

50
00:02:57,480 --> 00:02:59,380
What did the midwife have?

51
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:00,810
Position.

52
00:03:00,810 --> 00:03:03,340
This one is genuinely important.

53
00:03:03,340 --> 00:03:12,960
For thousands of years women gave birth upright — squatting, kneeling, or on a birthing
stool, which is a low wooden chair with a horseshoe-shaped hole in the seat.

54
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:18,300
Gravity helps, and the pelvis opens wider
in a squat than lying down.

55
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:24,270
Lying flat on your back is a much later fashion, and
it did not arrive because it was better for the woman.

56
00:03:24,270 --> 00:03:29,550
It arrived with male physicians, because it was
more convenient for the person doing the watching.

57
00:03:29,550 --> 00:03:31,340
She had her hands.

58
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:35,910
A skilled midwife could feel the position of
the baby through the abdomen and turn it.

59
00:03:35,910 --> 00:03:39,140
That skill was real, and it saved lives.

60
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:42,340
She had patience, and
permission to use it.

61
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:45,310
Labour was allowed to take
as long as it took.

62
00:03:45,310 --> 00:03:49,720
And she had a great deal of
ritual with no medical effect whatsoever.

63
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:56,250
Knots untied around the house, doors unlocked, girdles
and charms, herbal drinks that mostly did nothing.

64
00:03:56,250 --> 00:03:58,040
It's easy to laugh at that.

65
00:03:58,040 --> 00:03:59,210
Don't.

66
00:03:59,210 --> 00:04:06,380
Those things gave a terrified person something to do with their hands during
the worst hours of their life, and doing something is not nothing.

67
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:11,180
What she did not have was any answer at
all for the three things that actually killed people.

68
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:13,480
The first was obstructed labour.

69
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:16,420
The baby physically cannot pass.

70
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:20,180
Today that's a caesarean
and everybody goes home.

71
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:28,100
Then, it was days of labour ending in the death of the baby and
usually the mother, and there was no version of this with a good outcome.

72
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:30,110
The second was haemorrhage.

73
00:04:30,110 --> 00:04:34,000
Bleeding after the birth, which can
empty a person in minutes.

74
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,220
No transfusion, no drugs to
clamp the uterus down.

75
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:39,990
You could rub the abdomen and pray.

76
00:04:39,990 --> 00:04:41,910
Sometimes it stopped on its own.

77
00:04:41,910 --> 00:04:43,190
Sometimes it didn't.

78
00:04:43,190 --> 00:04:45,520
And the third killed more than either.

79
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:47,160
Infection.

80
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:48,830
Childbed fever.

81
00:04:48,830 --> 00:04:50,440
Puerperal fever.

82
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:52,860
A woman would deliver, and be fine.

83
00:04:52,860 --> 00:04:56,700
Talking, holding the baby, tired but well.

84
00:04:56,700 --> 00:05:02,710
And then two days later a fever would start,
and within a week she would be dead.

85
00:05:02,710 --> 00:05:08,800
That was the one that terrified people most, because it
came after the danger was supposed to be over.

86
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:13,140
And it is the one with the
most unbearable story attached to it.

87
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:19,520
The Vienna General Hospital had two maternity clinics, and pregnant
women in the city knew the difference between them.

88
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:22,990
The first clinic was staffed
by doctors and medical students.

89
00:05:22,990 --> 00:05:25,170
The second was staffed by midwives.

90
00:05:25,170 --> 00:05:28,930
Women arriving in labour would beg to
be admitted to the second one.

91
00:05:28,930 --> 00:05:33,250
Some gave birth in the street outside
rather than be taken into the first.

92
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:39,440
And they were right to: in the doctors' clinic,
around one woman in ten died of childbed fever.

93
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:45,250
In the midwives' clinic, it was closer to one
in twenty-five, and in good years better than that.

94
00:05:45,250 --> 00:05:46,700
Same hospital.

95
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:48,060
Same city.

96
00:05:48,060 --> 00:05:51,560
Same women, assigned
essentially at random.

97
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:55,560
A young doctor called Ignaz Semmelweis
could not let it go.

98
00:05:55,560 --> 00:06:00,860
He worked through every difference between the two
wards, and eventually found the one that mattered.

99
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:04,790
The doctors and the students
began each morning performing autopsies.

100
00:06:04,790 --> 00:06:08,280
Then they walked upstairs and
examined women in labour.

101
00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:11,110
They wiped their hands on
their aprons on the way.

102
00:06:11,110 --> 00:06:13,910
The midwives did no autopsies.

103
00:06:13,910 --> 00:06:17,850
Semmelweis had them wash their hands
in a chlorinated lime solution.

104
00:06:17,850 --> 00:06:24,420
The death rate in the doctors' clinic collapsed almost
immediately, down towards the midwives' numbers and below.

105
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:26,000
He had solved it.

106
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,800
Twenty years before anyone
understood that germs existed.

107
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:31,220
It did not go well.

108
00:06:31,220 --> 00:06:32,850
He had no theory.

109
00:06:32,850 --> 00:06:38,300
He could not say what was being carried on those
hands, because nobody yet knew there was anything to carry.

110
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:42,490
All he had was a number that fell
when people washed and rose when they didn't.

111
00:06:42,490 --> 00:06:45,590
And the message was,
unavoidably, an accusation.

112
00:06:45,590 --> 00:06:52,360
He was telling the most respected physicians in Europe that they
had been killing their own patients with their hands for years.

113
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:54,150
They did not take it well.

114
00:06:54,150 --> 00:07:04,310
His findings were dismissed, his contract was not renewed, and he grew increasingly furious and
erratic as the deaths continued in wards that had gone back to not washing.

115
00:07:04,310 --> 00:07:07,810
He wrote open letters
calling his colleagues murderers.

116
00:07:07,810 --> 00:07:11,360
In eighteen sixty-five he was
committed to an asylum.

117
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:16,690
He died there within two weeks,
aged forty-seven, of an infected wound.

118
00:07:16,690 --> 00:07:19,980
Germ theory was accepted
within a generation.

119
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:22,850
Everything he said turned out to be right.

120
00:07:22,850 --> 00:07:35,860
And there is a detail here that reframes the whole video, so sit with it: for a period of decades, going to
a hospital to give birth was substantially more dangerous than staying at home with a midwife who had never read a book.

121
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:38,560
Hospitals did not start out safer.

122
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:42,960
They started out as places where infection had
a lot of people to move between.

123
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:49,690
Put it all together and you get something that is not
really about pain, although the pain was total and unmedicated.

124
00:07:49,690 --> 00:07:52,030
It was about the settling of affairs.

125
00:07:52,030 --> 00:07:55,740
Women approaching a birth wrote letters
to be opened if they died.

126
00:07:55,740 --> 00:07:58,210
They arranged who would take the children.

127
00:07:58,210 --> 00:07:59,710
They made peace with people.

128
00:07:59,710 --> 00:08:05,630
In many places there was a formal blessing beforehand,
and afterwards a ceremony for having survived it.

129
00:08:05,630 --> 00:08:08,890
That is the part we've
lost the ability to feel.

130
00:08:08,890 --> 00:08:11,750
Not the agony — the arrangement.

131
00:08:11,750 --> 00:08:17,220
The fact that this was a thing you prepared
for the way you'd prepare for a dangerous voyage.

132
00:08:17,220 --> 00:08:27,030
And then, on the other side of it, in a warm dark room full of women who had been
there all night, most of the time — the overwhelming majority of the time — it was fine.

133
00:08:27,030 --> 00:08:29,420
That's the bit worth holding onto.

134
00:08:29,420 --> 00:08:33,450
It was survivable, it was normal, it
was done, and it was done again.

135
00:08:33,450 --> 00:08:39,660
Human beings walked into that room knowing the odds,
thousands of times each, for two hundred thousand years.

136
00:08:39,660 --> 00:08:42,010
That is where all of us came from.

137
00:08:42,010 --> 00:08:46,530
Every single person you have ever met
is downstream of somebody who did that.

138
00:08:46,530 --> 00:08:55,030
Today, in a wealthy country, the risk of dying in childbirth is somewhere
in the range of one in five thousand to one in twenty thousand.

139
00:08:55,030 --> 00:08:59,430
From one in a hundred to that,
in about a century and a half.

140
00:08:59,430 --> 00:09:06,310
Handwashing, antiseptics, transfusion, antibiotics, a caesarean
you can walk away from.

141
00:09:06,310 --> 00:09:12,740
It is one of the largest reductions in human suffering
that has ever happened, and almost nobody thinks about it.

142
00:09:12,740 --> 00:09:14,600
There is no holiday for it.

143
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:17,490
Most people could not name
a single person responsible.

144
00:09:17,490 --> 00:09:22,520
One of them died in an asylum,
insisting that everyone wash their hands.

145
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:30,050
And if you're now wondering what else in a hospital used to be
far more dangerous than it is today...
...that's a question for another video.
